# Page title standards

- Give every standalone public page one unique `<title>`.
- Format descriptive titles as `App Name — Descriptive Phrase [emoji]`.
- Use an em dash (`—`), not ` - ` or ` | `, as the section separator.
- Use title case for the app name and description. Keep ordinary short
  prepositions and articles lowercase.
- Use canonical product spelling consistently, such as `Pac-Man` and
  `Steel Drum`.
- Emoji are optional. When present in `<title>`, place one at the end.
- Keep titles near 60 characters or fewer. Shorten wording before removing
  the app name or its distinguishing search terms.
- Do not append `Joe Heyming`. The `/about/` page may include the name; the
  home page may include it when the page is specifically about the person.
- Base `og:title` and `twitter:title` on the document title. Keep product
  spelling and capitalization identical; prefer the exact title unless a
  social platform requires a shorter variant.
- When JavaScript changes `document.title`, keep the route's base title
  identical to the static `<title>`. Dynamic episode, file, or game-state
  context may be prepended.
- Redirect stubs, verification files, generated animation frames, and minimal
  legal pages do not need Open Graph or Twitter title metadata.
